00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:20.279
Do you ever wonder what it might take to make
a fish mad?

00:00:20.279 --> 00:00:24.910
Not just like a little irritated or cranky,
but fully leaping-out-of-the-water mad?

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:29.250
Like maybe you’ve done that thing you shouldn’t
be doing where you tap your fingers impatiently

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:33.929
at the glass of an aquarium just to get a
bit of a rise out of its residents.

00:00:33.929 --> 00:00:36.420
But they just glide on by like you don’t
even matter.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:47.269
You’re just a creature on the other side
of the glass, more provoked by a fish than it is by you.

00:00:47.269 --> 00:00:54.039
But let us suppose you wake up tomorrow, as
Kafka might say, from uneasy dreams, transformed

00:00:54.039 --> 00:00:57.609
in your bed into a monstrous vermin.

00:00:57.609 --> 00:01:04.600
Instead of the soft, malleable casing of your
skin, you are now armored with a rigid exoskeleton

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:09.880
made of chitin that you will periodically
shed and regrow.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:16.659
Your body is now an oval, with four pairs
of legs attached to the middle, thoracic region.

00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:19.060
And what does your head look like?

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.670
It looks weird.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.670
Almost a bit like a butt, right?

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:27.380
Except that structure at one end of you is
neither head nor butt.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.729
It is, for males, the testes.

00:01:29.729 --> 00:01:34.999
And for the females, it is the spermathecae,
a place to store sperm.

00:01:34.999 --> 00:01:37.140
This is your actual head.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.790
Much cuter, if you can call a head equipped
with suction cups and a needle cute.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:47.810
But it is a head, similar to your streamlined
body, that is built for one purpose.

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:58.939
Because you are a fish louse, and you are
about to provoke a fish.

00:01:58.939 --> 00:02:01.999
Now despite what the name suggests, you are
not actually a louse.

00:02:01.999 --> 00:02:04.079
You aren’t even an insect.

00:02:04.079 --> 00:02:10.369
You are one of more than 100 freshwater crustacean
species called Argulus.

00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:14.450
While you and your kind are found throughout
the world, some of your relatives likely got

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:16.740
their start long ago in Asia.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:21.840
They spread around the world as the trade
of carp and goldfish spread not only the fish

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.610
but their closest friends as well.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:28.780
Well, perhaps friends is not the right word.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:29.910
Parasite.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:31.430
That one that fits better.

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:34.160
Of course, the name doesn’t matter to you.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.390
Nor does your historical background.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:41.430
Why track some origin that’s ancient compared
to your lifespan when you probably don’t

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:44.830
even remember your own origins?

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:48.030
The mucus-coated egg laid in a white mass.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:52.380
Perhaps the weather was warm, and you spent
only a few days amongst the other eggs.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.290
Or maybe it was colder, and you waited weeks.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:02.159
And then when you hatched, you had many siblings—anywhere
from tens to hundreds of them.

00:03:02.159 --> 00:03:07.670
But you’ve gone your separate ways now,
in search of your next meal, and your next home.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:11.120
Two things that are, for you, the same thing.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:16.730
And if you find them, you have the chance
to live to the ripe old age of 60 days.

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:21.840
But if your search turns up empty, then survival
becomes impossible.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.480
And that means the situation is urgent, but
you do have a bit of time.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:33.290
Several days, potentially even up to two weeks,
to swim through the water and find the right fish.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.620
Fortunately, you are not particularly picky.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:37.569
Sure, you have your preferences.

00:03:37.569 --> 00:03:38.569
Who doesn’t?

00:03:38.569 --> 00:03:41.900
But at the end of the day, a meal is a meal,
and a fish is a fish.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:46.430
Sometimes, if you can’t find a fish, you’ll
even make do with a frog.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:51.739
To get there first, though, you have to swim,
paddling with your legs, and catapulting yourself

00:03:51.739 --> 00:03:56.129
from predators using lobes that attach to
your belly to launch yourself away.

00:03:56.129 --> 00:04:01.780
But when the opportunity—or fish—presents
itself, the main appendages at work are the

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:05.969
suction cups, or suckers, that you keep stuck
to your target.

00:04:05.969 --> 00:04:10.820
And should you need to move, the suckers on
their own are moveable stalks, meaning that

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:15.180
you can direct them independently to scurry
across your meal.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:19.640
Once you have suctioned yourself appropriately,
it’s time to feed.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:26.770
Your head comes equipped with a retractable
stylet, that thin tube poking out like a parasitic unicorn’s horn.

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:31.320
Through that stylet will travel whole blood
cells, ready to feed you.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.470
This is a content moment for you.

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:38.530
A fish louse needs only one fish in its whole
lifetime to sustain itself.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:43.280
And while this may not be the way everyone
would choose to live their life, who can argue

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:45.390
with being well-fed?

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:48.700
The fish, however, takes notice.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:54.450
As you take up residence on its skin and start
feeding on it, oh boy, does the fish take notice.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:57.220
And it will try to shake you.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:02.760
It will scratch its flank up against whatever
objects are around, whether that’s a plant or a stone.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.120
It will leap into the air.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:10.070
It might even jump onto the concrete of a
garden pond, risking predators and death as

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:15.920
it tries to get you off its back and out of
its skin.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:20.080
Perhaps it wouldn’t have made such a foolhardy
decision under normal circumstances.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:25.780
But in the fish’s defense, you have managed
to get just under its skin enough, poking

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:31.940
away with your stylet and proboscis and possibly
even secreting toxins and digestive enzymes

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:35.430
that feed you and irritate the fish.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:38.620
And no one is quite themselves when they’re
irritated.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:39.800
But you?

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:45.620
Well, you will just go on, unaware surely
of the damage you have caused, of the response

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:46.930
you have provoked.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:50.000
Because after all, to you, it wasn’t damage.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.960
It wasn’t meant to provoke.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:06:00.350
It was just one creature in search of food,
through whatever means were available to it.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:06.220
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:11.400
And we would like to also say thank you again
